  "narrative": {
    "intro": "New Mexico has $15B in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. The state recorded 9,837 awards with an average award size of $1.5M, and obligations are up 10.9% year over year. The current-year total is partial, covering fiscal year to date. Obligations have trended upward over the past several years: FY21 was $11.6B, FY22 was $11.7B, FY23 was $12.4B, FY24 was $13.1B, FY25 was $13.5B, and FY26 is $15B. The distribution of spending is concentrated: one agency and one industry account for most obligations, while a small set of vendors capture the largest vendor shares. This snapshot shows where federal money is concentrated in New Mexico during FY2026 to date and provides a quick view of the top buying agencies, leading vendors, and the largest NAICS industries by obligations.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies account for most federal obligations in New Mexico?",
        "body": "The Department of Energy obligated $10.6B, making it the largest buyer in New Mexico. The Department of Homeland Security obligated $2.1B and the Department of Defense obligated $1.1B. Several other agencies contributed smaller totals: Department of the Interior $438M, Department of Health and Human Services $299M, and Department of Agriculture $236M."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which vendors and industries capture the largest federal contract shares in New Mexico?",
        "body": "TRIAD NATIONAL SECURITY, LLC received $5.3B and NATIONAL TECHNOLOGY & ENGINEERING SOLUTIONS OF SANDIA, LLC received $5.2B, making them the largest vendors. FISHER SAND & GRAVEL CO received $1.8B while other top vendors include AMES FEDERAL CONTRACTING GROUP LLC $129M and APPLIED RESEARCH ASSOCIATES, INC. $108M. Facilities Support Services accounted for $10.5B in obligations and Commercial and Institutional Building Construction accounted for $2.1B, with Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences at $313M."
      }
    ]
  },
